{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-02-fractional-negative-decimal-indices",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Fractional, Negative, and Decimal Indices",
    "description": "Translate unusual-looking exponents into roots and reciprocals before doing the arithmetic.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat the exponent as an instruction card: the denominator tells the root, the numerator tells the power, and a minus sign tells us to take a reciprocal. Convert decimal exponents to familiar fractions instead of using a calculator.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach a^(1/n) as the nth root of a, a^(m/n) as an nth root combined with the mth power, and a^(−m) as 1/a^m. Connect terminating decimal exponents used in the chapter, such as 0.5, 0.25 and 1.5, to fractions before calculating. Cover negative exponents on fractional bases, where the base is inverted first, and decimal bases such as 0.25 and 0.04 that become easy powers of 1/2 or 1/5. Finish with expressions combining a fractional positive power and a negative fractional power. Learners should decide in this order: sign of exponent, denominator of exponent, then remaining power."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "An exponent does not have to be a whole positive number.\n\nA fraction in the exponent points to a root. A minus sign points to a reciprocal. A decimal exponent is usually just a familiar fraction wearing decimal clothes.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A square root can be written as a power.\n\n81^(1/2) asks: what number squared gives 81?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Because 9^2=81:\n\n81^(1/2)=√81=9\n\nThe denominator 2 in the exponent means square root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
